Here's another spoiler for 2.2a.  It's more interesting and more valuable
than the others I just posted.  I don't know if this has been debugged out
of the newer versions (I should expect it to have been), but at least you
can enjoy it in 2.2a.

Ready?

This is a method for identifying objects using 'c' and '\'.  Maybe this has
been posted before but in case it hasn't, I thought I'd share it, 'cause
it works on my version.

Whenever you get an item of the following kinds: potion, scroll, wand, or
ring, call its kind something with the sequence 'c' 'n'.  I usually use the
description to name it, like 'a glowing potion' becomes 'a potion called
Glowing'.  So what, you say?  Then look at your discoveries with '\'.  It
listed everything you know and includes these items you've 'called'
something.  The order that these discoveries are listed is the same every
time.  Since the Hack Tutorial was just printed, I can say that the order it
lists the different kinds of objects is the same order that '\' does.  Get
the picture?  You just keep 'c,n'ing things until you know what they must
be.  You can often get them down to a choice of two or three, but that's
better than not knowing what it will do at all.

Once you've figured out what they must be, you can 'c,n' them again right
away.  For example, suppose you hit '\', and you see 'a potion called
Glowing (glowing)' between 'potion of object detection (pink)' and 'potion
of gain strength (smoky)'.  Then you can call it 'a potion called Sickness
or Confusion', since you have it narrowed down to those two.

I tried this with spellbooks, but the order appears to be different than in
the Tutorial, and I don't run into them often enough for it to be handy
anyways.  I suspect that it works on gems too (I haven't tried it yet), but
if it does, I suspect the most valuable ones go to the top in the discoveries
list, so identify those first if contemplating the unicorn method of luck
increasing.

This is great is stores when you can't escape and can't pay either.  You can
just go around 'c,n'ing things to gather more info.  A decent-sized potion
store usually gives you a good idea what all the potions are before you've
tried any of them!  Then you can pay for (if you must), or steal, any that
you need, instead of guessing.

Anybody got any better ideas on what to call the items?  Something more
useful then 'a potion called Glowing '?

Oh yes, there is a bug associated with this method.  When you die, and your
items are identified, it doesn't identify the potions but keeps refering to
them as 'a potion called <such-and-such>'.  Just to let you know.

